title: Regex for security work, hands-on
guide: howto/regex-for-security.html
promise: A regex (regular expression) is a short pattern that matches text. Learn to write patterns you can defend, covering allowlist bypasses, credential scanning, regex engines and catastrophic backtracking.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is beginner to intermediate: you can use a terminal but need not have written a regex before. Allow about two hours.

You need grep and python3; ripgrep (rg), used in step 6, is optional. Reading a little Python helps.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks whole into your terminal; in Python steps, the lines down to PY run as a Python program. REGEX blocks hold patterns to read, not run; the PYTHON block is a line to study.

== Set up: ~/regex-lab and sample.log
section: Walk-through
You need a terminal with grep and python3. The walk-through's first block makes ~/regex-lab, a throwaway folder, and writes the files every step uses. This cat line writes the lines down to EOF into sample.log.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/regex-lab && cd ~/regex-lab

cat > sample.log <<'EOF'
2026-08-20T14:03:11Z user=alice ip=203.0.113.14 action=AssumeRole result=success
2026-08-20T14:03:12Z user=bob ip=198.51.100.7 action=GetObject result=denied
2026-08-20T14:05:40Z user=svc-deploy ip=10.0.4.19 action=PutBucketPolicy result=success
2026-08-20T14:06:02Z user=mallory ip=203.0.113.99 action=ListBuckets result=denied
2026-08-20T14:06:03Z user=mallory ip=203.0.113.99 action=ListBuckets result=denied
EOF
```

== Set up: secrets.txt, from the same block
section: Walk-through
The same block then writes secrets.txt, four lines, only two holding an AWS (Amazon Web Services) access key ID. Paste it whole, down to EOF. If a later command cannot find a file, run cd ~/regex-lab and try again.
```bash
cat > secrets.txt <<'EOF'
aws_access_key_id = AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE
temp_key = ASIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE
not_a_key = XAKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLEX
doc_reference = "the AKIA prefix identifies long-term keys"
EOF
```

== What a regex is and where you meet it
section: Why regex is a security topic and not a text-editing trick
A regex is a short pattern that describes which text to match. It becomes a control when it decides what is allowed or raises an alert: an allowlist (the addresses a service may fetch) or a scan for leaked keys.

== Two families of regex engine
section: Two engines, two sets of rules
A regex engine is the code inside a tool that runs a pattern. Backtracking engines, like Python's, can take exponential time on a hostile input. Finite-automaton engines, like Go's, always take about twice the time for twice the input.

== The one idea: too broad or too narrow
section: Too broad or too narrow: the two ways a pattern fails
Each pattern below should accept only HTTPS addresses on subdomains of example.com; .* is any run of characters, \. a literal dot, and anchors, ^ and $, pin the start and end. Too broad admits attacks; too narrow misses them.
```regex
# TOO BROAD - the dot is unescaped, so it matches ANY character
https://.*.example.com          # matches https://evilXexampleYcom

# TOO BROAD - no anchors, so the match can sit anywhere in the string
https://.*\.example\.com        # matches https://evil.com/?x=.example.com

# TOO NARROW - anchored and escaped, but assumes lowercase and no port
^https://[a-z]+\.example\.com$  # misses https://API.example.com:8443
```

== Step 1: anchors are a security control
section: 1. Anchors, and why they are a security control
In a search, an unanchored pattern is a convenience. In a validator, code that accepts or rejects input, it is a bug: "contains" is not "is". This one passes all three URLs: each prints True.
```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
import re
allow = re.compile(r'https://.*\.example\.com')      # no anchors
for url in ["https://api.example.com",
            "https://evil.com/?redirect=.example.com",
            "https://api.example.com.attacker.net"]:
    print(bool(allow.search(url)), url)
PY
```

== Step 2: Python's $ allows a trailing newline
section: 2. The Python anchor that is not an anchor
In Python, $ also matches before a trailing newline, so "prod-web\n" passes. That matters if the value lands in a header, a log line or a config file. For a validator, use \A and \Z, which admit no newline.
```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
import re
p = re.compile(r'^[a-z0-9-]+$')          # "a simple hostname label"
print(bool(p.match("prod-web")))          # True  - fine
print(bool(p.match("prod-web\n")))        # True  - surprising
print(bool(p.match("prod-web\nrm -rf /")))# False - the dot rule saves you here

q = re.compile(r'\A[a-z0-9-]+\Z')        # \A and \Z admit no newline at all
print(bool(q.match("prod-web\n")))        # False - what you meant
PY
```

== Step 3: greedy, lazy or explicit
section: 3. Character classes, quantifiers, and greed
Quantifiers like * are greedy by default: they take as much as they can. Adding ? makes them lazy, taking as little as possible.

Prefer the third: [^ ]+, everything up to the next space, is unambiguous.
```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
import re
line = 'user=alice action=AssumeRole result=success'
print(re.search(r'user=(.*) ',  line).group(1))   # greedy: 'alice action=AssumeRole'
print(re.search(r'user=(.*?) ', line).group(1))   # lazy:   'alice'
print(re.search(r'user=([^ ]+)', line).group(1))  # explicit: 'alice'  <-- prefer this
PY
```

== Step 5: scan for AWS access key IDs
section: 5. Write a credential scanner
An AWS access key ID is AKIA (long-term) or ASIA (temporary) plus 16 uppercase alphanumerics. With -E, grep uses extended regular expressions; -o prints only the match. The first also matches inside XAKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLEX; \b, a word boundary, rejects that.
```bash
grep -oE '(AKIA|ASIA)[A-Z0-9]{16}' secrets.txt
grep -oE '\b(AKIA|ASIA)[A-Z0-9]{16}\b' secrets.txt
```

== Step 5: where regex stops
section: 5. Write a credential scanner
The other half of an AWS credential pair, the secret access key, is 40 characters from the bracketed class. The pattern prints nothing here but matches most git hashes. Real scanners add checks. Regex finds candidates. It does not decide.
```bash
grep -oE '\b[A-Za-z0-9/+=]{40}\b' secrets.txt
```

== Step 6: lookaround, and engines without it
section: 6. Lookaround, and living without it
Lookaround checks text just before or after a match without including it; here, no uppercase letter or digit may precede AKIA. ripgrep's default engine has none, so the first command fails to parse. -P switches to PCRE2, where it works.
```bash
rg '(?<![A-Z0-9])AKIA[A-Z0-9]{16}' secrets.txt
rg -P '(?<![A-Z0-9])AKIA[A-Z0-9]{16}' secrets.txt
```

== Step 7: time a nested quantifier
section: 7. Catastrophic backtracking
Nested quantifiers over overlapping alternatives, then something that fails, make a backtracking engine explore exponentially many paths. Each extra character roughly doubles the time. Your numbers depend on your computer; what matters is how fast they grow.
```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
import re, time
evil = re.compile(r'^(a+)+$')          # nested quantifier, both greedy
for n in (18, 20, 22, 24):
    s = "a" * n + "!"                  # the "!" guarantees the match fails
    t = time.perf_counter()
    evil.search(s)
    print(n, f"{time.perf_counter() - t:.3f}s")
PY
```

== Step 7: why it matters, and three defences
section: 7. Catastrophic backtracking
An attacker who controls a validator's input can hang every worker running it. Defend in this order of preference:
- Use a finite-automaton engine for untrusted input.
- Avoid nested quantifiers over overlapping alternatives.
- Otherwise, cap input length before the regex.

== Exercise: get past this URL allowlist
section: Hands-on exercises
An internal service fetches a URL the user supplies and validates it with this allowlist. Give three distinct URLs that pass validation but reach a host you do not control. The guide hides each answer under Show the answer.
```python
re.search(r'https://.*\.internal\.example\.com', url)
```

== Answer: no anchors, three ways past
section: Hands-on exercises
Each passes because the pattern has no anchors:
- https://evil.test/?x=.internal.example.com puts the text in a query string.
- https://api.internal.example.com.evil.test/ makes it only the start of the real host.
- https://evil.test/#https://api.internal.example.com puts it in the fragment.
Parse URLs first, then compare the parsed host.

== Exercise: which of these can hang?
section: Hands-on exercises
Which is a catastrophic-backtracking risk on PCRE (Perl Compatible Regular Expressions), a backtracking engine, and which is safe? Say why for each. Here \w is a word character, \s whitespace, ? optional and {1,64} 1 to 64 times.
```regex
A:  ^(\w+\s?)*$
B:  ^\w+(\s\w+)*$
C:  ^(a|ab)+c$
D:  ^[a-z]{1,64}$
```

== Answer: A and C are dangerous
section: Hands-on exercises
- In A, the optional \s? lets a run of word characters split between quantifiers in exponentially many ways.
- In C, a and ab overlap.
- B is safe: each repetition starts with whitespace.
- D is safe: a bound caps the work.

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Anchor validators at both ends; Python needs \A and \Z.
- A bare . in a hostname matches any character.
- Test strings that should not match: a pattern matching everything passes every positive test.
- A lowercase-only detection misses the obvious evasion.
